 abstract=f'DNA degradation and sequence errors obstruct data retrieval by fragmenting strands and corrupting their sequence. We present DBGPS2, an extension of de Bruijn graph reconstruction that constrains successive k-mer coverages and offers query and two-pass counting Bloom filters. In the most fragmented sample of a 210,000-strand thermal-aging series, exact recovery increased from 48.6% with the DBGPS rule to 95.4% under matched k-mer settings, reaching 97.1% with shorter k-mers, without incorrect outputs. Across three high-error photolithographic libraries, exact recovery ranged from 70.02% to 98.43%, with complete recovery of two files. Benchmarks on DNA Fountain, the 607,150-design Organick File 16 library and a 176-day aging sample compared graph configurations with prefix clustering, multiple alignment and weighted voting. On the same retained molecules, the tested DBGPS2 configurations reconstructed strands {min(speeds):.1f}–{max(speeds):.1f}-fold faster than this implementation, with three computational repeats per point and format-specific quality endpoints. These timings exclude shared and native-format preprocessing and outer decoding. Query filters preserved output sets. Two-pass filtering reduced HT176 peak graph RSS from {no_bloom_memory:.1f} to {count_memory:.1f} GB, with additional counting work. Checksum-verified DNA Fountain recovery provided a separate complete-file endpoint. The comparison distinguishes coverage-aware path selection, the cost of per-cluster alignment and Bloom-filter resource tradeoffs.'
