 h.replace_text(find('The controlled reconstruction benchmark extends the resource analysis'),'The expanded comparison places reconstruction mechanisms at the centre of the resource analysis: clustering/MSA/voting is compared with DBGPS2 both without Bloom filtering and across two distinct Bloom mechanisms (Figs. 10 and 11). Avoiding a separate alignment for each retained prefix cluster can reduce reconstruction cost, while graph pooling can use overlapping fragments that this prefix-based baseline cannot assign through an intact index. This workflow advantage is inherited from graph reconstruction and should be distinguished from the incremental coverage-ratio rule. Query filtering preserves exact lookup decisions but adds construction and probe costs; two-pass filtering changes singleton support and trades counting work, graph size and subsequent traversal cost. Its speed must therefore be read together with recovery and incorrect-output counts. The HT176 benchmark uses successfully merged molecules and does not replace the full-raw-read thermal-pool measurements. The conventional baseline uses Python prefix grouping and per-cluster MUSCLE subprocesses, so process startup, communication and implementation choices contribute to its cost. These experiments do not reproduce all similarity-clustering or BMA implementations and do not establish universal, equal-recovery or full-outer-decoder speedups.')
 # Qualify the original thermal-only Bloom output agreement, without altering its data.
 h.replace_run_text(find('The singleton filter primarily reduced memory.'),'All reported thermal-pool Bloom configurations','All Bloom configurations in the original control, HT3 and HT5 resource experiment')
 h.replace_run_text(find('The Bloom filters address implementation cost independently'),'in the reported comparisons','in the original thermal-pool resource comparisons (Fig. 7)')
 h.replace_run_text(find('For the new comparison, all methods consumed'),'For the new comparison','For the photolithographic comparison')
 methods=[
 'For the expanded matrix, DNA Fountain and HT176 paired reads were merged with NGmerge 0.3 using eight threads, dovetail detection and default overlap/mismatch thresholds. Only successful merges were retained; Fountain additionally required 152 nt. HT176 was aged for 176 days at 70°C and sequenced separately from the HT1–HT5 series. Its local raw-read identities are specified by file size and checksum; the 2022 Song citation identifies the strand design, not a verified public accession for HT176. Organick used the full untrimmed read FASTA. Shared preparation and native-format view creation were performed before every reconstruction timer; their costs are documented separately. The Fountain graph interface added a synthetic constant boundary anchor and one synthetic terminal lookahead nucleotide without supplying payload sequence.',
 f'Graph modes used k = 21, 29 and 31 for Fountain, Organick and HT176. Primary DBGPS2 settings were (c, r) = (0, 6), (0, 1.9) and (1, 2.55). Additional no-Bloom settings used c = 1, 1 and {selected["song_ht176"]["dbgps_cutoff"]}, respectively, with the same ratios. The absolute-cutoff comparator used r = 1,000,000 and pilot-selected c = 1, 3 and {selected["song_ht176"]["dbgps_cutoff"]}. Selection used a deterministic hashed 1/128 seed subset, maximizing exact count for Organick/HT176 and RS-valid count for Fountain. The candidate grid was 1, 3, 5, 10 and 20, plus 25 and 40 for HT176. This retrospective tuning was excluded from timing and supplies no held-out generalization estimate.',
 'The Bloom matrix used B8, B16, F32, F34 and B12/F34 at the fixed primary search settings. B is the minimum query-filter bits-per-key budget; actual allocations are rounded to powers of two and recorded in bytes. F32 and F34 request total first-pass bitmap budgets of 512 MiB and 2 GiB. The format caller invokes the same existing counter and Bloom APIs as the CLI. Both counting passes, singleton pruning, query-filter construction, seed handling, bounded search, validity checks and output writing are included. Query filters preserve exact-table fallback after positive probes. Two-pass counting discards singleton k-mers; output equivalence to an unfiltered graph was tested rather than assumed. Native graph-process peak RSS was taken from /usr/bin/time -l in bytes, and the maximum across three repeats is plotted. MSA worker memory was not summed and is not included in that memory comparison.',
 'The conventional pipeline grouped by exact observed prefixes: 16 nt for Fountain and HT176, and 14 nt after normalizing Organick molecules to one orientation. HT176 used the same numeric index range and 15-nt primer suffix as graph seed construction. At least three observations were required, with up to 15 reads per alignment and seed 20261019. Fountain and Organick used uniform reservoir sampling. HT176 preferentially retained reads with the longest observed coverage of the known 164-nt region, breaking ties randomly; terminal alignment gaps were missing observations, while internal gaps retained weight 0.4. MUSCLE 3.8.31 was followed by weighted voting and the same format-specific validity rules as graph candidates: zero-syndrome RS(38,36), the rotating-ternary predicate, or legacy mapping/CRC16. Conflicting valid prefixes were rejected without references.',
 'All 81 matrix entries are complete measurements: three computational repeats of nine configurations on three inputs. Processes ran serially with scheduled cyclic method order; completed unchanged comparator runs from the earlier rotated-order block were retained. Eight graph-count insertion workers plus pipelined reading/extraction and single-threaded search were compared with eight single-threaded MSA workers plus coordinator overhead. Total active threads and CPU utilization were not constrained to equality. Background applications remained present, no CPU affinity or forced cold-cache regime was imposed, and pre-run process snapshots were retained. Timers ended after validation, reference-free conflict rejection and sorted output writing, before truth scoring or outer decoding. SHA256 checks established repeat agreement and query-filter output preservation. Separate Fountain outer-decoding checks and re-encoding comparisons used the checksum-verified archive; original synthesis-seed-list membership was not inferred.'
 ]
