 add_figure(10,'Fig10_reconstruction_speed_Bloom_matrix.png','Fig. 10 | Reconstruction speed of clustering/MSA/voting and graph configurations. a, DNA Fountain. b, Organick File 16. c, The 176-day HT176 sample using the Song strand design. Bars show mean reconstruction time after common and native-format preprocessing, with sample s.d. from three serial computational repeats; axes are logarithmic. Numbers above bars are speed ratios relative to the conventional baseline. The DBGPS rule is emulated within the same core. DBGPS2 uses no Bloom filter at two coverage thresholds, query filters B8/B16, counting filters F32/F34, or B12/F34 together. Bloom cases retain the primary c and r within an input. B is query bits per key and F is log2 total counting-bitmap bits. Table 6 reports output quality; Figure 11 reports graph memory and yield changes. Timings include filter construction or both counting passes, seeding, reconstruction, validation and writing. Shared preprocessing, tuning, scoring and outer decoding are excluded. All methods use the same retained molecules; HT176 includes successful merges only. These are implementation-specific operating-point comparisons.','Three panels compare conventional clustering and MSA with DBGPS rule and seven DBGPS2 search/Bloom configurations, with timing standard deviations and speed ratios.')
 add_figure(11,'Fig11_Bloom_memory_and_recovery.png','Fig. 11 | Graph memory and output changes across coverage and Bloom settings. a, Maximum native graph-process resident set size across three computational repeats, in decimal GB; counting-filter bitmap allocation and query-filter construction are included. MSA process-tree memory is not estimated from the per-process time report. b, Change in output yield relative to the primary no-Bloom DBGPS2 point within each dataset. Fountain values count RS-zero-syndrome droplets with distinct seeds; Organick and HT176 values count exact complete encoded regions. These endpoints are distinct. Query-filter outputs were identical to the no-Bloom outputs, and F32, F34 and B12/F34 agreed with one another. Singleton removal can alter yield relative to the unfiltered graph. The additional no-Bloom cutoff and DBGPS-rule rows also change search acceptance, as specified in Methods. Absolute quality and erroneous-output counts are in Table 6. All repeated output sets within a configuration were identical.','Heatmaps report graph peak RSS and output-count changes relative to primary no-Bloom reconstruction, including query and two-pass counting filters.')
